Usage: convex-local-backend.exe [OPTIONS] [DB_SPEC] [COMMAND]

Commands:
  keygen  Generate keys without starting the server
  help    Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Arguments:
  [DB_SPEC]
          File path for SQLite, the file path; for postgres, a server URL
          
          [default: convex_local_backend.sqlite3]

Options:
  -d, --db <DB>
          Database driver type
          
          [default: sqlite]
          [possible values: sqlite, mysql-v5, mysql-v5-multitenant, postgres-v5]

  -i, --interface <INTERFACE>
          Host interface to bind to
          
          [default: 0.0.0.0]

  -p, --port <PORT>
          Host port daemon should bind to
          
          [default: 3210]

      --site-proxy-port <SITE_PROXY_PORT>
          Host port to bind for Convex HTTP Actions
          
          [default: 3211]

      --convex-origin <CONVEX_ORIGIN>
          Origin of the Convex server, as accessible from the client. e.g. if the client is running on localhost, you can use the default of http://127.0.0.1:${port}. Otherwise, if the client is accessing the backend from the public internet, this would be the public URL of the backend, like https://api.my-app.com . Note the port in this url (usually 443, the https default) need not match the bind port `--port`

      --convex-site <CONVEX_SITE>
          Origin of the Convex HTTP Actions, as accessible from the client. e.g. if the client is running on localhost, you can use the default of http://127.0.0.1:${site_proxy_port}. Otherwise, if the client is accessing the backend from the public internet, this would be the public URL of the backend, like https://my-app.com . Note the port in this url (usually 443, the https default) need not match the bind port `--site-proxy-port`. If you don't have a separate URL for HTTP Actions, you can use value from `--convex-origin` with a "/http" suffix, like https://api.my-app.com/http 

      --convex-http-proxy <CONVEX_HTTP_PROXY>
          Optional proxy for Actions fetches
          
          i.e. if doing `await fetch(request)` within an action, you can send the request through this proxy to screen it for SSRF attacks.

      --instance-name <INSTANCE_NAME>
          Instance name for this backend

      --instance-secret <INSTANCE_SECRET>
          Instance secret for this backend

      --local-storage <LOCAL_STORAGE>
          Which directory should file storage use
          
          [default: convex_local_storage]

      --s3-storage
          Use S3 storage instead of local storage

      --do-not-require-ssl
          If set, the persistence won't require SSL when talking to the database. It would still prefer SSL if available. This should only be set in tests

      --disable-beacon
          self-hosted Convex will periodically communicate with a remote beacon server. This is to help Convex understand and improve the product. If set, the self-host beacon will not be sent
          
          [env: DISABLE_BEACON=]

      --redact-logs-to-client
          If set, logs will be redacted from clients. Set this on production deployments, to prevent information like stacktraces of serverside code from being leaked to clients.
          
          On development deployments, it can be helpful to have this information reach the client for debugging purposes.

      --local-log-sink <LOCAL_LOG_SINK>
          Path of local file for logs to be routed to. For local testing of log integrations (eg axiom/datadog)

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version
